CDN (Content Delivery Network)
What is CDN?

A Content Delivery Network (CDN) is a geographically distributed network of servers that stores copies of static content closer to users. Instead of every request traveling to the origin server, users receive content from the nearest CDN edge location.
-------------------------------------------
Why is CDN Needed?

Without CDN, a user in the USA accessing a server hosted in Mumbai experiences high latency.

With CDN:
--------

User
  ↓
Nearest CDN Edge Server
  ↓
Origin Server


This significantly reduces response time.

Benefits
--------
Lower latency
Faster page load
Reduced backend load
Improved user experience
Better global performance
Popular CDN Providers
AWS CloudFront
Akamai
Cloudflare
Azure CDN
Banking Use Cases
Online banking portal images
Monthly account statements (PDFs)
Customer reports
Static web assets

Interview Answer

"CDN improves application performance by caching static content at edge locations closer to users, reducing latency and minimizing traffic to backend services."
